<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Neak Pean</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Neak Poan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Neak Pean, the entwined serpents, is an artificial island at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> Buddhist temple on a circular island in the Jayatataka <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baray">Baray</a>, the reservoir of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preah-khan">Preah Khan</a> temple, built in the reign of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a>. Its name comes from the sculpted nagas around the base of the temple, and some historians believe it represents Anavatapta, a mythical Himalayan lake whose waters cure illness. It was one of the many hospitals Jayavarman VII built: four pools, standing for water, earth, fire and wind, were fed from the main tank through conduits shaped as heads. The temple was dedicated to Avalokitesvara, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-daguan">Zhou Daguan</a> mentioned it in the late 13th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/neak-pean.jpg" alt="Neak Pean" width="330" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wetstew · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neakpeancentralpond2014.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the reign of Jayavarman VII alone Ta Prohm, Preah Khan, Banteay Kdei and <b>Neak Pean</b> were built, beside the Bayon at the centre of Angkor Thom.</blockquote><a href="/societies/khmer-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Khmer Empire</a></li></ul></section>
